  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Anatomy of an IPv4 Address",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Address = 32-Bit Integer",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "IPv4 = 32-bit unsigned int, four dotted octets",
            "192.168.10.37 = 0xC0A80A25 = 3232238117",
            "Octet range 0-255; 256.1.1.1 is not an address",
            "2^32 = 4,294,967,296 total — the root of exhaustion",
            "Scarcity is exactly why NAT exists"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import ipaddress",
            "a = ipaddress.ip_address('192.168.10.37')",
            "print(int(a))   # 3232238117",
            "print(hex(int(a)))  # 0xc0a80a25"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Bit Weights In One Octet",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Bit",
            "Weight",
            "Bit",
            "Weight"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1",
              "128",
              "5",
              "8"
            ],
            [
              "2",
              "64",
              "6",
              "4"
            ],
            [
              "3",
              "32",
              "7",
              "2"
            ],
            [
              "4",
              "16",
              "8",
              "1"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Memorize these eight numbers. Every subnet computation falls out of them."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Conversion Drill",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "10.1.200.5 -> 00001010.00000001.11001000.00000101",
            "200 = 128 + 64 + 8 -> 11001000",
            "Convert one octet a day until it is reflex"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "print(format(10, '08b'))   # 00001010",
            "print(format(200, '08b'))  # 11001000",
            "print(format(5, '08b'))    # 00000101"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Network Bits vs Host Bits",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Leftmost bits = network, shared by all hosts on the link",
            "Rightmost bits = host, unique per host",
            "Split point chosen by the designer, not by the address",
            "That freedom IS classless addressing",
            "A bare address cannot route: an interface has 10.0.0.5/24"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Vocabulary",
          "text": "Prefix = network portion. Host bits = the remainder. The pair (address, prefix length) naming a block is called a prefix, or a route."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import ipaddress",
            "n = ipaddress.ip_network('10.4.0.0/14')",
            "print(n.netmask)  # 255.252.0.0",
            "print(n.num_addresses)  # 262144"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Anatomy of an IPv4 Address (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import ipaddress",
            "a = ipaddress.ip_address('10.1.2.7')",
            "n = ipaddress.ip_network('10.1.0.0/16')",
            "print(a in n)  # True"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. The Classful Era",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Class",
            "Lead bits",
            "Split",
            "Range"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "A",
              "0xxx",
              "/8",
              "1.0.0.0-126.255.255.255"
            ],
            [
              "B",
              "10xx",
              "/16",
              "128.0.0.0-191.255.255.255"
            ],
            [
              "C",
              "110x",
              "/24",
              "192.0.0.0-223.255.255.255"
            ],
            [
              "D/E",
              "1110/1111",
              "-",
              "224-239 mcast, 240-255 reserved"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Why Classes Broke",
          "items": [
            "Class A = 16,777,214 hosts; Class C = 254",
            "A site needing 500 hosts burned a whole Class B",
            "Crisis 1: exhaustion from wasted Class B space",
            "Crisis 2: routing explosion of un-aggregatable /24s"
          ],
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. CIDR Removes The Classes",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "RFC 1519 in 1993, current spec RFC 4632",
            "Prefix length is carried explicitly with the address",
            "Any length 0-32 is legal",
            "Notation A.B.C.D/n, n = leading network bits",
            "Example: 172.16.8.0/22, no class involved"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Two Powers It Unlocked",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "VLSM: subnets of one org can differ in prefix length",
            "Supernetting: four adjacent /24s advertised as one /22",
            "Result: the global routing table stopped exploding"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Classful Habits Still Leak",
          "text": "Some routers default an unmasked route to its classful length, and old exam questions assume /24 for 192.x. Always state the prefix."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Class letters are historical vocabulary only",
            "Never let a letter decide a mask"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Anatomy of an IPv4 Address (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. The Mask Is 32 Bits",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Contiguous 1s for network, then contiguous 0s for hosts",
            "/24 = 255.255.255.0, /26 = 255.255.255.192",
            "Prefix length and dotted mask: one fact, two notations",
            "255.255.0.255 is illegal — non-contiguous masks are dead"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Mask Table, One Octet",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "/n",
            "Value",
            "/n",
            "Value"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "/25",
              "128",
              "/29",
              "248"
            ],
            [
              "/26",
              "192",
              "/30",
              "252"
            ],
            [
              "/27",
              "224",
              "/31",
              "254"
            ],
            [
              "/28",
              "240",
              "/32",
              "255"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Octet Boundaries",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Prefix",
            "Dotted mask"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "/8",
              "255.0.0.0"
            ],
            [
              "/16",
              "255.255.0.0"
            ],
            [
              "/24",
              "255.255.255.0"
            ],
            [
              "/32",
              "255.255.255.255 (one host)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Wildcard And Host Bits",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Wildcard = 255.255.255.255 minus the subnet mask",
            "/26 mask 255.255.255.192 -> wildcard 0.0.0.63",
            "Wildcards appear in ACLs and OSPF statements",
            "Host bits h = 32 - n; block size = 2^h addresses"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import ipaddress",
            "n = ipaddress.ip_network('192.168.10.0/26')",
            "print(n.hostmask)  # 0.0.0.63"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Block Size At A Glance",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Prefix",
            "Addresses",
            "Usable"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "/24",
              "256",
              "254"
            ],
            [
              "/25",
              "128",
              "126"
            ],
            [
              "/26",
              "64",
              "62"
            ],
            [
              "/27",
              "32",
              "30"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "/28 = 16/14, /29 = 8/6, /30 = 4/2",
            "Bigger blocks: /23 = 510 usable, /22 = 1022",
            "/21 = 2046, /20 = 4094, /16 = 65534"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "From Classful to CIDR",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Longer Prefix, Smaller Block",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "More network bits means fewer host bits",
            "/25 is HALF of /24, not double — classic reversal",
            "/24 gives 254 usable, /25 gives 126"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Magic Number",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "increment = 256 - mask octet value",
            "/26 -> 256-192 = 64 -> .0 .64 .128 .192",
            "/28 -> 256-240 = 16 -> .0 .16 .32 .48 ...",
            "Network = address AND mask; the mask is a bitwise filter"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Three Kinds Of Address",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Network = all host bits 0; names the block in the table",
            "Broadcast = all host bits 1; reaches every host on link",
            "Usable = everything strictly between the two",
            "Usable count = 2^h - 2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Five-Second Sanity Check",
          "text": "Is the network address a multiple of the block size? If it is not, the notation you were handed is wrong."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "h = 32 - 26        # host bits",
            "print(2**h)        # 64",
            "print(2**h - 2)    # 62"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Worked: 192.168.10.37/26",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "AND 255.255.255.192",
              "192.168.10.0"
            ],
            [
              "OR wildcard 0.0.0.63",
              "192.168.10.63"
            ],
            [
              "Usable range",
              ".1 - .62 (62 hosts)"
            ],
            [
              "Next subnet",
              "192.168.10.64"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import ipaddress",
            "n = ipaddress.ip_network('192.168.10.0/26')",
            "print(n.broadcast_address)  # 192.168.10.63"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "37 in binary is 00100101, so the top 2 bits are 00",
            "That puts .37 inside the very first /26 block",
            "Block size 64 -> blocks at .0 .64 .128 .192"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Worked: 172.16.135.9/20",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Mask 255.255.240.0, increment 16 in the third octet",
            "135 lands in the 128 block -> network 172.16.128.0",
            "Broadcast 172.16.143.255",
            "2^12 - 2 = 4094 usable hosts"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. The Odd Prefixes",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "/31 (RFC 3021): 2 addresses, both usable, no broadcast",
            "/31 exists for point-to-point links",
            "/32 = single host route: loopbacks, host routes, BGP next-hops"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Directed Broadcast Is Dropped",
          "text": "A packet aimed at a remote subnet's broadcast address is normally discarded by routers — mitigation for the Smurf attack."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Network, Broadcast, Usable Range",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Range Gotchas",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Classic off-by-one: forgetting the -2 entirely",
            "Never subtract 2 on a /31 — both addresses are usable",
            "Never hand a host the network or broadcast address",
            "The gateway eats one more: /29 = 6 usable, 5 for hosts"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import ipaddress",
            "n = ipaddress.ip_network('10.0.0.0/29')",
            "print(len(list(n.hosts())))  # 6"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Subnetting = Borrowing",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Take bits from the host portion, give them to the network",
            "Borrow b bits and you create 2^b subnets",
            "Each subnet then holds 2^(h-b) addresses"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Split 192.168.1.0/24 By 4",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Borrow 2 bits -> /26 -> block size 64",
            "Blocks: .0/26, .64/26, .128/26, .192/26",
            "Scales anywhere: 10.0.0.0/8 -> 256 /16s or 65,536 /24s"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Block",
            "Usable",
            "Broadcast"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              ".0/26",
              ".1-.62",
              ".63"
            ],
            [
              ".64/26",
              ".65-.126",
              ".127"
            ],
            [
              ".128/26",
              ".129-.190",
              ".191"
            ],
            [
              ".192/26",
              ".193-.254",
              ".255"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import ipaddress",
            "n = ipaddress.ip_network('192.168.1.0/24')",
            "s = list(n.subnets(new_prefix=26))",
            "print(s[1])  # 192.168.1.64/26"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Sizing Rule",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "To fit H hosts, choose h where 2^h - 2 >= H",
            "100 hosts needs h=7 (/25); 2^6-2 = 62 is too small",
            "There is no /24.5 — always round up to a power of two",
            "Asking for 300 hosts costs a /23 (510 usable)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Hosts",
            "Host bits",
            "Prefix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "6",
              "3",
              "/29"
            ],
            [
              "30",
              "5",
              "/27"
            ],
            [
              "100",
              "7",
              "/25"
            ],
            [
              "300",
              "9",
              "/23"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. VLSM Plan On 172.16.0.0/22",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Need",
            "Block",
            "Prefix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "500 hosts",
              "172.16.0.0",
              "/23"
            ],
            [
              "200 hosts",
              "172.16.2.0",
              "/24"
            ],
            [
              "60 hosts",
              "172.16.3.0",
              "/26"
            ],
            [
              "P2P links",
              "172.16.3.192",
              "/30s"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Allocate largest first, carve smaller from what remains",
            "Wrong order strands blocks nothing can use"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Borrowing Bits and VLSM",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Alignment Is Law",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A /26 must start at a multiple of 64 in its octet",
            "192.168.1.100/26 is a host address, not a network",
            "Valid-subnet check: address AND mask == the address"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Aggregation Runs It Backwards",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Merge adjacent blocks by shortening the prefix",
            "192.168.0.0/24 + 192.168.1.0/24 = 192.168.0.0/23",
            "Legal only if adjacent AND the merged block is aligned"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "The Merge That Fails",
          "text": "192.168.1.0/24 + 192.168.2.0/24 cannot become a /23. The /23 containing .1.0 is 192.168.0.0/23, so it swallows .0.0 and misses .3.0."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Finding The Aggregate",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Write the candidate networks in binary",
            "Count the common leading bits",
            "That count is the aggregate prefix length"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Worked: Four /16s",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "10.4.0.0, 10.5.0.0, 10.6.0.0, 10.7.0.0 all /16",
            "Second octet 00000100 .. 00000111 differs in 2 bits",
            "13 common leading bits -> 10.4.0.0/14"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "for o in (4, 5, 6, 7):",
            "    print(format(o, '08b'))  # 000001xx"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Why ISPs Aggregate",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Allocations sit on power-of-two boundaries to stay mergeable",
            "Aggregation keeps the global BGP table from exploding",
            "0.0.0.0/0 is the ultimate aggregate: zero network bits"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Over-Aggregation Black-Holes",
          "text": "Advertise a /14 while owning only three of the four /16s and traffic for the fourth is swallowed and dropped."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Number hierarchically: region -> site -> VLAN",
            "Flat random allocation can never be summarized later"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. RFC 1918 Private Space",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Block",
            "Span",
            "Addresses"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "10.0.0.0/8",
              "10.0.0.0-10.255.255.255",
              "16,777,216"
            ],
            [
              "172.16.0.0/12",
              "172.16.0.0-172.31.255.255",
              "1,048,576"
            ],
            [
              "192.168.0.0/16",
              "192.168.0.0-192.168.255.255",
              "65,536"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "172.32.x.x is PUBLIC — a very common misread",
            "Private space is not globally routable; ISPs drop it",
            "So reaching the internet requires NAT"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import ipaddress",
            "n = ipaddress.ip_network('172.16.0.0/12')",
            "print(n[-1])  # 172.31.255.255"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Supernetting and Special Ranges",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. More Reserved Blocks",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Block",
            "Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "127.0.0.0/8",
              "Loopback — the whole /8"
            ],
            [
              "169.254.0.0/16",
              "Link-local APIPA: DHCP failed"
            ],
            [
              "100.64.0.0/10",
              "CGNAT, RFC 6598"
            ],
            [
              "192.0.2.0/24",
              "Documentation, RFC 5737"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Doc ranges: 192.0.2.0/24, 198.51.100.0/24, 203.0.113.0/24",
            "Use them in write-ups, never a real customer's IP",
            "CGNAT looks private but sits outside RFC 1918"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Loopback is the whole 127.0.0.0/8, not one address",
            "100.64.0.0/10 sits between subscriber and ISP NAT",
            "Doc and CGNAT space is not RFC 1918 space"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Spot The Failed Lease",
          "text": "An interface holding a 169.254.x.x address never got DHCP. Check the server before you debug routing at all."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Last Special Blocks",
